// PA-v0.21-009 (#1285): TlbOps typed wrapper — invlpg / invpcid / wbinvd.
//
// Trait TlbOps exposes the three TLB / cache invalidation primitives as
// typed stdlib helpers so callers do not have to hand-roll unsafe blocks
// with raw mnemonics. Consumed by paideia-os R18-M5 (TLB shootdown IPI
// handler) and R22 (VT-d IOMMU flush after invalidation queue drain).
//
// Semantics (Intel SDM Vol 3A §4.10, §11.5):
//   invlpg(va)                       Invalidate every TLB entry (and
//                                    corresponding paging-structure cache
//                                    entry) that translates the linear
//                                    address `va`. Local to the executing
//                                    CPU — TLB shootdown across cores
//                                    requires an IPI.
//   invpcid_single(pcid, va)         Invalidate TLB entries for `va`
//                                    scoped to `pcid` (INVPCID type=0).
//                                    Descriptor: 128-bit [pcid:64][va:64]
//                                    with pcid in the low 12 bits.
//   invpcid_all_nonglobal(pcid)      Invalidate all TLB entries for
//                                    `pcid` except global pages
//                                    (INVPCID type=1). Descriptor
//                                    low 12 bits carry pcid; high bits
//                                    ignored by hardware but zeroed here.
//   flush_cache_writeback()          WBINVD — write back and invalidate
//                                    every line of every cache. Very
//                                    expensive (millions of cycles on
//                                    modern parts); typically only run
//                                    at power-state transitions or when
//                                    resetting MTRRs.
//
// Privilege: every method is Ring-0 only. INVLPG / INVPCID / WBINVD
// #GP(0) at CPL > 0.
//
// Effect discipline:
//   invlpg / invpcid_* are !{sysreg, mem} — they mutate the TLB, which
//   is architecturally part of the system-register set.
//   flush_cache_writeback is !{sysreg, mem, cache} — it additionally
//   serializes globally-observable cache state, and downstream code that
//   depends on visibility across cores must not reorder around it.
//
// Capability: @{paideia.sysreg} — the same kernel-only capability that
// gates cr3 writes and IDT loads.
//
// NOTE (2026-08-11): invlpg_single is landed with a lowering recipe today.
// invpcid_single / invpcid_all_nonglobal are declared but their lowering
// recipes wait on the INVPCID mnemonic landing across the exhaustive
// Mnemonic-match sites (encoder + IR passes + AST/IR round-trips) — see
// paideia-as#1285 comment thread for the follow-up sub-issue. Calling
// them today emits a normal-call diagnostic (unresolved intrinsic); no
// silent fall-through. flush_cache_writeback lowers to WBINVD directly.

trait TlbOps {
  fn invlpg_single(va: u64) -> () !{sysreg, mem} @{paideia.sysreg};
  fn invpcid_single(pcid: u16, va: u64) -> () !{sysreg, mem} @{paideia.sysreg};
  fn invpcid_all_nonglobal(pcid: u16) -> () !{sysreg, mem} @{paideia.sysreg};
  fn flush_cache_writeback() -> () !{sysreg, mem, cache} @{paideia.sysreg};
}
